<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="integration-spherical-complete">
    <title>Integration in Spherical Coordinates (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Spherical coordinates are important in describing relationships in quantum mechanics (eg., orbitals). 
                        We'll use what we've learned about the Jacobian to practice integrating in spherical coordinates towards
                        some applications we've seen previously (eg., normalizing the square of wave functions).
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Jacobian for Spherical Coordinates</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                Recall spherical coordinates result from the transformation <m>x=\rho \sin(\varphi)\cos(\theta)</m>, <m>y= \rho \sin(\varphi)\sin(\theta)</m>, <m>z=\rho \cos(\varphi)</m>.
                So the Jacobian for this transformation is
            </p>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle J = x_{\rho}(y_{\theta} z_{\varphi} - y_{\varphi} z_{\theta}) - x_{\theta}(y_{\rho} z_{\varphi} - y_{\varphi} z_{\rho}) + x_{\varphi}(y_{\rho} z_{\theta} -y_{\theta}z_{\rho}) </m>
            </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        which simplifies to <m>-\rho^2 \sin(\varphi)</m>. So <m>dV = |-\rho^2 \sin(\varphi)| = \rho^2\sin(\varphi) dV' </m>, 
                        as long as <m> 0 \leq \varphi \leq \pi</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="3in">
                <title>Examples: Jacobian for Spherical Coordinates</title>
             
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \iiint_S zx \quad dV</m>, where <m>S</m> is the
                            surface inside <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 =4</m> and <m>z= \sqrt{x^2 + y^2}</m>.
            
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Use the <url href="https://www.desmos.com/3d/qcgjhaukgw">integration in spherical coordinates Desmos visual</url> to 
                            visualize the surface.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            We have the bounds <m>0 \leq \rho \leq 2</m>, <m>0 \leq \theta \leq 2\pi</m>, and 
                            <m>0 \leq \varphi \leq \pi/4</m>. The upper bound of <m>\varphi</m> comes from transforming 
                            <m>z = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2}</m> to <m>\rho \cos(\varphi) = \rho \sin(\varphi)</m>, which implies
                            <m>\tan(\varphi) = 1</m>, which implies <m>\varphi = \pi/4</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            With these bounds, the integral becomes 
                            <md>
                                \int_0^{\pi/4} \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^2 \rho \cos(\varphi) \rho\sin(\varphi)\cos(\theta) \rho^2\sin(\varphi) \quad d\rho d\theta d\varphi
                            </md>.
                            
                        </p>
                    </solution>

          
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise workspace="3in">
        <title>Normalization</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
              Suppose <m>F(\rho, \theta, \varphi)</m> is the square of a wave function that needs to be normalized. What is 
              the relevant triple integral?
            </p>
           
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                 <md>
                     \int_0^{\pi} \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^\infty F(\rho, \theta, \varphi)\rho^2\sin(\varphi) \quad d\rho d\theta d\varphi
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>

        <exercise workspace="5in">
        <title>Integrals Over the Surface of a Sphere</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                There will be applications when it is desired to integrate just over the <em>surface</em> of a sphere. 
                This can be accomplished by holding <m>\rho</m> constant:
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \int_0^{\pi} \int_0^{2\pi} f(\theta, \varphi) \sin(\varphi) \quad d\theta d\varphi
                </md>
                integrates <m>f(\theta, \varphi)</m> over the surface of the unit sphere (<m>\rho = 1 </m>).
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>
    
    </page>


       
    </worksheet>
